{# "Edit this bit of the chrome", shown on the bit itself.

   The navbar, the banner and the footer are the three things people ask how to change, and
   until now the answer was a route or a page name you had to be told (ticket 30 exists
   because of exactly that). A pencil where the thing is says it without being told.

   The label is written out, not hidden in a tooltip. A bare pencil says "something here is
   editable" and stops there, while the whole point is to name *which* piece of chrome it
   opens -- "Éditer le bandeau" answers the question the pencil raises. It can afford the
   room because it is not on screen until the region it belongs to is hovered (see
   `.yw-chrome-edit` in yw-core.css), so it never competes with the site's own navigation.

   No `title` and no `aria-label`: the visible text is the accessible name, and either
   attribute would only repeat it -- `aria-label` would additionally override the text a
   sighted user reads with a copy of itself.

   It is only rendered for someone who may follow it -- TemplateEngine checks before this
   template is reached, so there is no disabled state to explain.

   `yw-btn` as well as `yw-chrome-edit`: these are the same control as the floating action
   button in the corner, so they are the same button -- same shape, same weight, same
   quiet-until-reached-for treatment. Four affordances that do one kind of thing should not
   look like four different ideas. #}
<a class="yw-btn yw-chrome-edit" href="{{ href }}">
  {{ icon('pencil') }}<span class="yw-chrome-edit__label">{{ label }}</span>
</a>
